+(durations)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may appear at most once, units must be
+given in that order, and spaces are not allowed. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`.
+
+Negative values, decimals, unknown units, and repeated or out-of-order units are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta`
+passed directly, for example as a `default`, is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
